From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Alex <306578489+hourlysword@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 19:24:46 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] ARM: dts: sun8i: v3s: Add the CPU's performance counters

The V3s device tree has no node for the Cortex-A7's performance
monitoring unit (PMU), so perf only offers software events. Add it, so
hardware events such as CPU cycles and instructions can be counted and
sampled.

The overflow interrupt is SPI 120: the V3s uses the H3's interrupt
numbers for the devices both device trees describe (DMA, MMC, USB,
UART, I2C, SPI, timer, watchdog, codec, Ethernet), and SPI 120 is CPU0's
PMU interrupt on the H3. cpu@0 gets a label for interrupt-affinity.

Tested on a FunKey S: the armv7_cortex_a7 PMU registers with 5
counters, sampling every million cycles raises one SPI 120 interrupt
per sample, and an 8-second count reaches 7.5 billion cycles (past
2^32; about 94% of the 1008 MHz clock, the rest going to other tasks),
so counter overflows are caught.
---
 arch/arm/boot/dts/sun8i-v3s.dtsi | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm/boot/dts/sun8i-v3s.dtsi b/arch/arm/boot/dts/sun8i-v3s.dtsi
index 42e5b3b..8d73cd2 100644
--- a/arch/arm/boot/dts/sun8i-v3s.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm/boot/dts/sun8i-v3s.dtsi
@@ -68,7 +68,7 @@
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 
-		cpu@0 {
+		cpu0: cpu@0 {
 			compatible = "arm,cortex-a7";
 			device_type = "cpu";
 			reg = <0>;
@@ -93,6 +93,12 @@
 		status = "disabled";
 	};
 
+	pmu {
+		compatible = "arm,cortex-a7-pmu";
+		interrupts = <GIC_SPI 120 IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_HIGH>;
+		interrupt-affinity = <&cpu0>;
+	};
+
 	timer {
 		compatible = "arm,armv7-timer";
 		interrupts = <GIC_PPI 13 (GIC_CPU_MASK_SIMPLE(4) | IRQ_TYPE_LEVEL_LOW)>,
